        <div id="porch-zones" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. One Porch, Four Different Paint Jobs
          </h2>

          <p>
            It helps to think of a porch as four separate surfaces, each with its own product. The floor takes foot traffic, standing water and sun. The steps take the same, plus the most slips and the hardest wear at the nosing. The railings, columns and trim are vertical exterior woodwork. The ceiling is sheltered but humid and prone to mildew. One gallon of anything will not serve all four well.
          </p>

          <p>
            The floor and steps need a porch and floor enamel, the subject of this guide. These are harder and more abrasion resistant than wall paint and are formulated to survive wet shoes, dragged chairs and freeze-thaw. The steps usually need the same enamel plus traction, either from an anti-slip additive or a textured coating.
          </p>

          <p>
            Railings, columns and trim are best served by a good exterior trim enamel, usually satin or semi-gloss, which sheds water and cleans easily. Using floor enamel on vertical trim is not harmful, but the sheen and color may not match the house trim, and trim enamels flow out more smoothly on spindles and moldings.
          </p>

          <p>
            The ceiling is a separate decision entirely, and we cover it in its own section below. If your porch is concrete rather than wood, our guide to the <a href="/best-concrete-floor-paint/">best concrete floor paint</a> covers slab-specific products in more depth, and if your porch is really an open deck, our <a href="/best-deck-paint/">best deck paint</a> picks are better suited to exposed horizontal boards with no roof overhead.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
