        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which paint belongs on a porch floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Pick a porch and floor enamel rated for your surface, wood or concrete. Water-based acrylic enamels are the most common choice today because they stay flexible on wood, resist fading and clean up easily. Low-luster or satin sheens hide wear and are less slippery than gloss. Avoid ordinary wall or siding paint, which is too soft for foot traffic.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a wood porch need primer before painting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Prime any bare wood, including sanded spots, new boards and patched areas, with an exterior primer suited to the wood and porch use. Sound, previously painted surfaces usually need only cleaning and sanding. Priming bare wood improves adhesion and helps block tannin bleed in species like cedar and redwood. Follow the paint manufacturer's primer recommendations.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should porch paint dry before walking on it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most porch enamels are ready for light foot traffic in about 24 hours, but full cure takes longer, often a week or more depending on temperature and humidity. Wait several days before returning furniture and longer before placing rugs or mats, especially rubber-backed ones. Cool or damp weather extends these times.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use oil-based or latex paint on a porch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most porches today are best served by water-based acrylic latex enamel. It stays flexible on wood, resists cracking and fading, cleans up with water and has low odor. Oil-based enamels dry harder but become brittle and are less common due to VOC limits. If your porch already has oil paint, prepare it properly before switching to latex.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I make a painted porch less slippery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mix an anti-slip additive into the final coat, broadcast fine grit into the wet paint, or use a textured coating on steps and landings. Choose low-luster or satin rather than gloss. Stir additive-filled paint often during application so the grit stays evenly distributed. Focus extra traction on steps, ramps and entry areas.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I paint a concrete porch with porch paint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Many porch and floor enamels are rated for both wood and concrete. Test for sealers and moisture, clean and degrease, and etch smooth concrete before painting. New concrete should cure at least 28 days. Use the primer or first-coat method the label recommends, and add traction to steps and areas that get wet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often does a porch need repainting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A quality porch enamel on a well-prepared surface commonly lasts about three to five years, depending on traffic, sun and weather. Steps and high-traffic areas wear sooner. Rolling a maintenance coat when traffic lanes look thin, before the paint wears through, extends the life of the finish and saves a full strip-and-repaint later.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

